abstract: >-
  Research package (reproducible pipeline, confirmatory protocol and figure code) for
  a study of small-parcel coffee boundary delineation in Minas Gerais, Brazil (2017),
  using CONAB/fiboa br_conab reference parcels. Identical Random Forest and linear
  probes predict boundary probability from seasonal Sentinel-2, seasonal Sentinel-1
  plus Sentinel-2, the AlphaEarth annual embedding and their fusion, per pixel and
  with the same four neighborhood features (local mean, local standard deviations, gradient)
  for every input; watershed and region-merging decoders extract parcel objects. The
  neighborhood-feature control was fixed in a written protocol and tested on 48 new
  tiles from 36 unused regions: per pixel the embedding ranks boundary pixels better
  (Boundary AP 0.579 vs 0.449), but with identical neighborhood features seasonal Sentinel-2
  exceeds the embedding
  at exact one-pixel boundary localization (0.664 vs 0.610; Hodges-Lehmann difference -0.052, 95% CI
  -0.063 to -0.043); with a one-pixel tolerance the two tie. In this setting, the per-pixel baseline overstated the embedding's advantage. Includes
  reference extraction, tile sampling, Earth Engine download, probing, decoding,
  paired statistics (non-superiority, TOST), the protocol with the ordered
  confirmatory tile list and frozen thresholds, per-tile results, and figure generation. Intermediate
  data are regenerable from the code with fixed seeds.
keywords:
  - Field boundary delineation
  - foundation model embeddings
  - Sentinel-2
  - spatial context
  - AlphaEarth
